From a40c3abc8c3b9383579d8a3cd2b9c4d6eb56a26d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Tom Leger <thomas@leger.run>
Date: Sun, 30 Aug 2026 04:30:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] models: a catalog row for the FP8 TP=2 checkpoint, so the
 sync stops deleting 185.6 GB off both twins
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

This row is the fix, and the only fix: an artifact the manifest wants is
an artifact the prune keeps.

  lib/local-models.nix   the artifact row itself, in the shape
                         deepseek-v4-flash-0731-bf16 established:
                         layout = "snapshot" (a 131-shard HF tree must
                         not collapse to basenames), localName for
                         loaders that select by repository basename,
                         pinned revision 970c569a, primary config.json,
                         and one entry per file with exact bytes + the
                         LFS sha256 + its SRI. 141 files, 131 shards,
                         185,563,713,216 bytes.

  modules/strix.nix      the artifact moves out of the coordinator-only
                         `lib.optionals` and is listed for BOTH twins.
                         Tensor parallelism shards the compute, not the
                         checkpoint: each box loads the whole thing from
                         its own NVMe.

No deployments row, deliberately. Serving stays with the flashnext pair
service (github.com/mecattaf/flashnext), which is a vLLM TP=2 process
across the two boxes, not something llama-swap can start; and promotion
onto any fleet roster is a separate, human act. This commit changes only
which bytes the twins are entitled to keep.

Facts read from the Library copy at
/mnt/nas/models/weights/qwen38-flash-next-fp8 (staging had not re-run
yet): bytes from stat, oids from the huggingface_hub download metadata
(the LFS etag IS the content sha256 — verified by recomputing eleven
shards spread across the set, all matching). The eleven small non-LFS
files carry a git blob sha1 as their etag, so their sha256 was computed
directly.

Before this lands, rename the Library directory to match the artifact id
(`mv /mnt/nas/models/weights/{qwen38-flash-next-fp8,flashnext-fp8}` on
the NAS) — both library-fetch and local-models-sync address the Library
as `weights/<artifactId>/`, and without the rename the 02:30 fetch timer
would re-download 173 GiB it already has.
---
 lib/local-models.nix | 862 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 modules/strix.nix    |  13 +-
 2 files changed, 874 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/lib/local-models.nix b/lib/local-models.nix
index beb8fc38..16ee8a93 100644
--- a/lib/local-models.nix
+++ b/lib/local-models.nix
@@ -1113,6 +1113,868 @@ let
               };
             };
 
+            flashnext-fp8 = {
+              kind = "model";
+              maker = "Qwen";
+              notes = "flashnext TP=2 lane: the vendor FP8 release (125B trunk + 51.2B hash-lookup embedding, 6B active, 262144 context), 185.6 GB REQUIRED IN FULL ON EACH TWIN — tensor-parallel shards compute, not the on-disk checkpoint. Declared as an artifact, not a deployment: vLLM serves it through the flashnext pair service (github.com/mecattaf/flashnext, host/fn-cluster-up.sh), never as a llama-swap row. THIS ROW IS THE ANTI-PRUNE: without it local-models-sync rm -rf's /var/lib/local-models/flashnext-fp8 on every boot, rebuild, and sync start — it did exactly that on both twins on 2026-08-29, costing a 185.6 GB per-node re-stage. Apache-2.0 weights, governed by the upstream model license.";
